KPL/LSK

AUTHORED BY FARSIGHT. THIS IS NOT A NAIF PRODUCT.

A minimal leapsecond kernel used as a test fixture, so that CI can exercise the SPICE time path
without redistributing a NAIF kernel. ADR-016 rejects a curated FarSight kernel bundle (its
Option 7) and requires every real kernel to be fetched by hash from a publisher URL recorded in a
DataArtifact; its own Enforcement item 2 uses "text kernels authored by FarSight" for exactly
this purpose, which is what this file is.

The constants below are definitional or published facts rather than NAIF's work:

  DELTA_T_A   32.184 s, the definitional TT - TAI offset.
  K, EB, M    the periodic relativistic terms of the TDB - TT series, whose values are given in
              the SPICE documentation and in the Explanatory Supplement to the Astronomical
              Almanac. They are reproduced because a leapsecond kernel without them will not
              load, not because they are ours.
  DELTA_AT    the last two entries of the IERS leap-second table. The full table runs from 1972;
              only the tail is included, which is what makes this a fixture and not a substitute
              for naif0012.tls. Epochs before 2017-01-01 are outside it -- see the warning below,
              because they do NOT fail.

Verified equivalent to naif0012.tls for the DSOC benchmark epoch: both give
deltet(2024-06-24T00:00:00, "ET") = 69.1843030063424 s.

READ THIS BEFORE USING IT FOR ANY OTHER EPOCH. SPICE does NOT refuse an epoch before the table
below. It extrapolates from the earliest entry and returns a plausible, wrong number, silently:

    epoch                 this fixture      naif0012.tls      error
    1980-01-01            67.183918 s       51.183918 s       16 seconds
    2000-01-01            67.183913 s       64.183913 s        3 seconds
    2024-06-24            69.184303 s       69.184303 s        none

A 16-second time error propagates into a trajectory as thousands of kilometres and looks entirely
normal on the way through. This fixture is valid ONLY for epochs on or after 2017-01-01, and
nothing in SPICE will tell you when you leave that range -- which is the concrete argument for
ADR-016's `kernel_coverage_completeness` being a freeze-time validator rather than a runtime
check.

\begindata

DELTET/DELTA_T_A       =   32.184
DELTET/K               =    1.657D-3
DELTET/EB              =    1.671D-2
DELTET/M               = (  6.239996D0   1.99096871D-7 )

DELTET/DELTA_AT        = ( 36,   @2015-JUL-1
                           37,   @2017-JAN-1 )

\begintext

End of the FarSight-authored fixture.
